se we only have  6%  edie visits and so it learns ET visits  are very very rare and they're probably  not going to happen  so I'm best off just hedging my bets and  saying you're not going to go to the  emergency department that's not what we  want and so um there's kind of two ways  that we can do that we can change  function parameters or the way I chose  to do we can change what data it's  trained on so what I did for that is I  created a separate data set that has  2,000 observations 1,000 of which did  not go to the emergency room and 1,000  of which did so I tricked the model into  thinking emergency room visits happen  about half of the time and so you should  learn what causes people to go to the  emergency room and this does cause it to  suffer in terms of accuracy but as we  see it'll help us in sensitivity and so  accuracy is while a great metric is not  the one we're primarily concerned with  we're concerned with positive predictive  value and sensitivity so positive  predictive value tells us of the things  that we have classified as positive how  many were actually positive so this is  our confidence that if we say you're  high risk you are actually high risk  which is important because we only want  to give tablets to those who are high  risk and then sensitivity which is how  many of the high risk patients did we  actually catch so I'll be talking about  five models we have a full  representative model which uses all  variables at our disposal and uses a  representative set of the data we split  our data into a training and testing  to avoid overfitting as much as we can  80% of our data was in the training set  and 20% in the testing set so that was  trained on the full 80 percent training  set a full even model which uses all of  the variables and uses my special even  data set in order to condition it a  reduced representative model which uses  a reduced subset of variables and the  way we reduced those as we looked which  specifically which HCC diagnosis codes  are most highly correlated with  instances of emergency room Department  and we took the top ten of those and  included those in our model and we did  the same for lDA's which are lion  strains and Airways and for labs  and for cancers and we used a set of  demographic information so that was  trained on all the data the reduced used  the same set of reduced variables and  used the altered even subsets and then  my artificial neural network which I  call an used a further reduced set of  variables because artificial neural  networks can only handle numerical or  complex vector arguments and so if you  try to put in a categorical variable it  will just cry and give up so the results  I listed out the true positive true  negative false positive and false  negative if you're interested we can see  our accuracies are pretty good the two  full and the two representative models  have above that 94% accuracy which is  exactly what we're looking for so these  are doing better than a naive model but  we can look at their sensitivity and see  it's about 10% which is pretty bad and  their positive predictive values are not  what we would like either the even on  the other hand have a slightly lower  accuracy but still pretty decent at 82%  but we can see their sensitivity gets  much better with 61 percent accuracy and  58 percent in the other case and then  the artificial neural network has  slightly worse accuracy slightly better  sensitivity is there's kind of a nice  middle-of-the-road model  so going forward I mentioned integrating  these into the reporting workbench what  that is is in epic we can put in a model  with all of our data manipulation in  order to turn raw input from physicians  into an output of a prediction of a risk  and there's a few problems here that  we're gonna run into and that is what  the epic servers are programmed to  handle and what they're compatible with  so they're compatible with all linear  models all logistic models and simple  random forest models so notably  artificial neural networks are not yet  programmed int to be put into the  reporting workbench so unfortunately an  is kind of useless to us at this point  and the more complicated random forest  models are just not ready to be put into  the epic workbench because they're  they're too complicated they're too  computationally expensive and doing so  would just bog down the whole system and  once we do that we'll move on to patient  trials in which they'll actually be used  and we'll say okay which are the highest  risk patients and we'll send them home  with tablets and we'll see if that  actually helps them and hopefully it  will says some thank yous the national  library of medicine training grant of  course the department of medical  informatics including clinical  epidemiology as well as i'm peter graven  and his team and the care management  plus team down at 30-30 moody who have  so graciously accommodated me in their  office even though i do some pretty  different work from them they've been  great colleagues and helped quite a bit  both my presentation poster research  techniques and the like thank you  [Applause]  hey thank you a great presentation  um one specific thing I was wondering  about you said that they're only 20  tablets have you tried looking at what  happens if you give like the top 20  highest risk well give you know if you  pretend that you had done that with the  20 that were labeled as being highest  risk and you see how many of them  actually did end up coming back to the  IDI how many of them did you miss yes  thank you actually I forgot to mention  that we did look at of the top 20 that  the model classifies which ones have the  highest accuracy I didn't include it  here but the accuracies were much lower  than what it stated in the accuracy for  the complete model but still on the  higher side between 50 and 75 percent  for all of them which it's up to the  institution if that's an acceptable  level of risk of catching 25% not  actually high risk patients and so  that's adds up to people who are not me  but it does have some some promising  aspects to it it's the best and why um  it really depends on your thought  process of what you prioritize if you  prioritize prioritize acts accuracy  above all else the reduced  representative model is going to be your  best bet and 96.7 percent accuracy if  you're valuing sensitivity so catching  the most number of actual high-risk  patients for instance if we have say a  larger number of tablets in the future  and we want to make sure we help as many  of the high-risk patients I would say  that the full even model would be the  best because it catches the highest  percentage of high-risk patients it  depends what you prioritize well it  seems like you have a metric because you  only have 20 tablets right now to do  this study with so it would seem that  the right metric would be you know the  precision at 20 which yeah you know how  in which case it would be the full  representative a 75% accuracy and you  said you get so although at 20 I guess  all the predictions are positive  yeah so all the predictions are positive  and 15 actually went to the emergency  department so 75% accuracy that's  probably pretty good I don't know Peter  can give you an economic analysis if  that makes it worth doing this study but  it sounds like that would be reasonable  to me right and models even get close to  that um the reduced representative had a  little over 50 percent which is not bad  again better than sticking a cat and  saying here decide who gets a tablet but  ideally you'd want the highest accuracy
 

